<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=library-of-ashurbanipal">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Library of Ashurbanipal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Royal Library of Ashurbanipal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-nineveh">Library of Ashurbanipal</a> is a collection of more than 30,000 clay tablets and fragments of the 7th century BCE, gathered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> from 668 to 627 BCE. He had cities and centres of learning across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> send him copies of their texts, sent scribes to collect them and took tablets as war loot; the texts, mostly Akkadian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, were placed in rooms by subject and marked with coloured signs or their first words. The fire that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-nineveh">destroyed Nineveh</a> in 612 BCE baked the tablets, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>. Most are now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/library-of-ashurbanipal.jpg" alt="Library of Ashurbanipal" width="330" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Todd · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Library_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>668–627 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Nineveh, Mesopotamia</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> · <a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>A similar library was found in 1849 in the palace of Sennacherib (705–681 BCE), and because the tablets of the two were mixed after reaching Europe, the contents of each can hardly be reconstructed.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform<span>c. 3300 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=library-of-ashurbanipal">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
